#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125649} :activity/id #uuid "ec39ccfa-c0bc-5c06-b1ec-f08770e57898" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125889 :tutorial/content "The **critical points** of a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ are the points $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist. A critical point such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ is called a **stationary point**.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x,y) = x^2 - 2x + y^2+6y.$ The domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x-2,\n\\qquad\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y+6.\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/9e/9ea3f2d1dd0994be64602aeaffcf5d8424f52daa03844e21e65469f779d9b009.svg)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that ![](images/23/23faa4c01362cad11c2ab6ad19045ca44a09460da1c719c8212480f27f21c5b1.svg) or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist:\n\n- Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2 &=0 \\\\ 2y+6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $x=1$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, $(1,-3)$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\n- $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so there are no more critical points to be found.\n\nTherefore, the only critical point of $f$ is $(1,-3).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66c2c3d5-6a0a-561b-81c6-ba81419caa61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "59e0e71c-41de-59e8-af18-15eaf180ea00" :step/math-academy-id 45414 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124216 :question/id #uuid "593eeb42-56b8-5177-8a9f-38d08a5186c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12584"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511549}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c1edc476-8e7a-5e8a-83d5-126f47cd2bfb" :step/math-academy-id 45415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125890 :tutorial/content "Recall that, for functions of two variables $f(x,y)$, there are typically three possible types of critical points that can occur:\n\n- Local minimum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ has a minimum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n- Local maximum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = -x^2-y^2$ has a maximum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/008803f43cb2161624f8ef295c70a125b8be64d93184928bd2176a6debaa9f85.png)\n\n- Saddle points. For example, the hyperbolic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2$ has a saddle point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/1214b413073ad87a302fb1297cef47208a922e368d680a9f69ec8fb18d9ca8a0.png)\n\nLocal maximum and local minimum points are analogous to what you would have seen in single-variable calculus. \n\nA saddle point occurs when the slopes are zero in all directions, yet it is not a local maximum or minimum. The hyperbolic paraboloid shown above reaches a local maximum at $(0,0)$ along the $xz$-plane, yet this is a local minimum along the $yz$-plane." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe3c5f00-f720-5573-a01f-c37f0a3e447f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7877 :tutorial/title "Classifying Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "c5093fcf-6163-5240-9022-125813bd45bf" :step/math-academy-id 45418 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8248e727-7421-5535-ba58-b5ed98f7ac38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7878 :tutorial/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "f1821258-7802-571e-a84f-0738986e608c" :step/math-academy-id 45419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124217 :question/id #uuid "7c9f4b9e-be60-5da1-a140-08a3f183ce0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520514}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "4f254984-e6a9-5246-b99d-34252f2d1d87" :step/math-academy-id 45420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124218 :question/id #uuid "e5398577-d70c-56f9-9c89-4a9d4e8f1d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-12586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "9c6b333c-1f09-554a-8089-06e97c78f009" :step/math-academy-id 45423 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125895 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of a function $f(\\mathbf x).$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that if $H(\\mathbf{a}) = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) \\neq 0$, then we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe second derivatives test follows from these facts. To see why, we first recall Sylvester's criterion:\n\nThe quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 > 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$ and\n\n- negative definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 < 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ are the first and second principle minors of $A,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice the following:\n\n- The first principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta_1 = (\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}))_{11} = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}.\n$$\n\n- The second principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is the Hessian determinant\n\n$$\n\\Delta_2 = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) = H(\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can go through each case:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 < 0.$ Hence, by Sylvester's criterion, the Hessian matrix $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive definite nor negative definite, and we conclude that $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 > 0.$ Then:\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} < 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} > 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bcd9133-9025-5637-8956-90a3b84934a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7886 :tutorial/title "Relating the Second Partial Derivatives Test to the Taylor Polynomial Classification"} :step/id #uuid "408587e1-74bc-5208-80a7-5977f2bc1961" :step/math-academy-id 45457}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124735}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520567} :activity/id #uuid "e57ebb2c-da7f-5557-85ec-a93c9bfb4862" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520056} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520145} {:answer/id #uuid "862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520187} {:answer/id #uuid "d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104994" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520031} {:answer/id #uuid "23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} {:answer/id #uuid "2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520058} {:answer/id #uuid "4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520088} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} :answer-field/id #uuid "40aea253-69a0-5d33-b3f4-8166b4cfbd4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1fd6c16-60ce-56fa-b03a-b1f876d9acf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37590" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(x+y+1) & 2(x+3y+5)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1 &=0 \\\\ x+3y+5 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n2y+4=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+(-2)+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,-2)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+3y+5))=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+3y+5))=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(1,-2))=2\\cdot 6-{2}^{2}=8>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,-2)=({f}^{″}(1,-2))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c27e9b4e-206f-5150-a881-d37dde9c4e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434966} {:answer/id #uuid "d2284ea7-6bfd-52fc-b08e-cbc5dc03d189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,1)$ and a local minimum $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434969} {:answer/id #uuid "e02c6dc0-e019-5937-b18e-930a68cb954f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434971} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c6c0-0532-521e-b2c3-2747f7ac1033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,1)$ and a saddle point at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434972} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d5c20b-2412-5e45-a4e7-034876616c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8b2aa5-8481-5045-bb5b-59aa8a2d0aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a336a18-c506-59a2-baef-25499733a8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81179" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3 &=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114d029d-432f-5d2d-a8e0-da4352b9cbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520012} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520137} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1bf858-df10-5d12-842f-190930cca500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520195} {:answer/id #uuid "a61d9d31-5126-5495-bb34-7b540584d0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520217} {:answer/id #uuid "da384a82-fb82-524f-a94b-1ea107ebde1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520137} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b332ce-1536-5642-87d8-20b5ef880b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e71f4d1b-7ab2-518d-aa98-4d69ed0f6542" :question/math-academy-id "q-37592" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y &=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520050} {:answer/id #uuid "3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520080} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520275} {:answer/id #uuid "ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105213" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,1)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f"}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519996}]}
